# hermes stack — manual setup

## Bootstrap env file

```bash
cp hermes.example hermes.env
chmod 600 hermes.env   # keep secrets out of git blame world-readable
```

Open `hermes.env` and fill in:
- **`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD`** — pick anything for basic auth (the username is already a placeholder).
- **`HERMES_API_SERVER_KEY`** — replace the placeholder with an actual secret:
  ```bash
  openssl rand -hex 32
  ```

## First run

```bash
mkdir -p ~/storage/homelab/homelab-conf/hermes/state
cd ~/homelab/containers
./up-all-stacks.sh hermes
```

Check it comes up healthy:

```bash
docker compose -f hermes/docker-compose.yml ps
docker compose -f hermes/docker-compose.yml logs -f hermes-gateway
```

The container will run the **interactive setup wizard** on first invocation.
It prompts for your model provider API keys and writes them into
`homelab-conf/hermes/state/.env` (the volume-mounted config). Example:

```bash
docker exec hermes_gateway /opt/hermes/.venv/bin/hermes setup
```

Set at least one key you also use in `openclaw.env` so both agents can access
your preferred model provider (e.g. `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`,
or similar).

## Reverse proxy (nginx-proxy-manager)

Add two proxy hosts in the NPM admin UI (see
`containers/nginx-proxy-manager/.setup` for how to reach it):

### API server (`hermes.smugiwara.com`)

- Domain: `hermes.smugiwara.com`
- Forward to: `hermes_gateway:8642`
- Scheme: `http`
- **Websockets Support: on** (long-polling also works for some clients)
- Advanced nginx snippet — require API key auth:
  ```
  # Require Bearer token for every request
  if ($http_authorization !~ "^Bearer ") {
      return 401;
  }
  # Validate the token from openclaw.env (both share the same stack)
  set $valid_token no;
  if ($http_authorization ~* "Bearer (.*)") {
      set $token $1;
  }
  ```
- Request a new SSL certificate (HTTP-01, same as every other proxy host)

### Dashboard (`hermes-dashboard.smugiwara.com` or same domain under /dashboard)

- Domain: `hermes-dashboard.smugiwara.com` (or use an NPM per-host path under the main domain)
- Forward to: `hermes_gateway:9119`
- Scheme: `http`
- **Websockets Support: on**
- Basic auth is already enforced by env vars — no extra nginx snippet needed.

## CLI usage (optional profiles / one-off tasks)

```bash
# List profiles, create sessions, check gateway status:
docker exec hermes_gateway hermes status

# Start a chat session in the default profile:
docker exec -it hermes_gateway hermes

# Create a second named profile (separate SOUL/memory/skills):
docker exec hermes_gateway hermes profile create work

docker exec hermes_gateway hermes -p work gateway start
```

## Sanity check

```bash
# API server endpoint (inside the container):
docker exec hermes_gateway wget -qO- http://127.0.0.1:8642/healthz

# Dashboard auth page (outside, via loopback after NPM is set up):
curl -sI https://hermes.smugiwara.com/v1/models | head -5
```

## Model shared with OpenClaw?

Hermes reads API keys from `/opt/data/.env` inside the container — this is
the file produced by `hermes setup`, _not_ the compose env_file. It is safe to
set the same model provider keys in both `openclaw.env` and `hermes/env` since
they live on the host as separate files. The compose-level `HERMES_API_SERVER_KEY`
is a service-specific token; keep it distinct from both.
